 All four honor `--json`. Exit codes:
 
 - `0` — success (an empty `migrate list` is a success with zero rows).
-- `1` — failure: transport or daemon error, or an apply/rollback that started
-  and could not finish (the receipt stays open; see below).
-- `2` — refusal: the daemon answered but declined the operation. In `--json`
-  mode the refusal result is printed to stdout first, so a script can read
-  `supported`/`reason` or `ok`/`reason` and still branch on the code.
-
-**Continuation.** A migration that dies between `apply` and its seal leaves an
-*open receipt* — `migrate list` shows it with `SEALED` = `open`. The next
-`autosk migrate apply <workflow>` does not re-plan: it finds the open receipt
-and finishes exactly that migration (re-planning over the remaining tasks
-would name a different migration). The same holds for an interrupted
-`rollback`. `apply` refuses rather than guess when several receipts are open,
-when `--from` does not match the open receipt's source, or when the workflow
-no longer serves the receipt's target — restore the target distribution or
-resolve the open receipts first.
-
-**Refusals** (exit `2`). `plan` and `apply` decline when:
+- `1` — failure: the daemon did not answer with a decline. A transport
+  error or a daemon that died mid-call, a filesystem error such as EACCES,
+  a corrupt runtime index or receipt, or a command run outside a project.
+- `2` — refusal: the daemon answered but declined the operation, whether it
+  declined before any write or after an apply/rollback had already begun
+  moving tasks. In `--json` mode the refusal result is printed to stdout
+  first, so a script can read `supported`/`reason` or `ok`/`reason` and
+  still branch on the code.
+
+Either class can leave a migration receipt open — the receipt's being open
+says the operation did not finish, not which of the two the command
+returned.
+
+**Continuation.** A migration that dies between `apply` and its seal leaves
+an *open receipt* — `migrate list` shows it with `SEALED` = `open`. The next
+`autosk migrate apply <workflow>` does not re-plan: it finds the open
+receipt and finishes exactly that migration (re-planning over the remaining
+tasks would name a different migration). The same holds for an interrupted
+`rollback`. A decline can also arrive while the receipt is open — the store
+re-checks every task's pin, document, shape and helper as it moves and on
+read-back — and it leaves the receipt open the same way: once the cause is
+removed, the next `apply` continues the same migration. `apply` refuses
+rather than guess when several receipts are open, when `--from` does not
+match the open receipt's source, or when the workflow no longer serves the
+receipt's target — restore the target distribution or resolve the open
+receipts first.
+
+**Refusals** (exit `2`). `plan` and `apply` decline when planning finds:
 
 - no open tasks of the workflow are pinned to a distribution and `--from` is
   not given — `no open tasks pinned to a distribution for workflow <w>`;
 - the open tasks are pinned to more than one distribution — `open tasks are
   pinned to N distributions for <w>; name --from`;
-- the source digest is not recorded — `source distribution <d> is not recorded`;
-- the distribution the workflow serves is not recorded — `target distribution
-  <d> is not recorded`;
+- an open task's runtime identity pin is unreadable — `cannot read the pin
+  of N open task(s) (<ids>)`;
+- a task's pin and its position name different workflows — `N open task(s)
+  name different workflows in their pin and their position (<ids>)`;
+- the open tasks on the source were admitted under more than one document —
+  `open tasks on <d> came from N documents (<ds>); a migration can only
+  claim one source`;
+- the source digest is not recorded — `source distribution <d> is not
+  recorded`;
 - the workflow has no registered distribution — `<w> has no identified
   distribution to migrate to`, or `<w> is not registered`;
+- the document the workflow serves is malformed — `<w> serves a document
+  that is not a lowercase sha256 ("<d>")`; the document prints JSON-escaped,
+  so a value with control characters stays on the operator's line (this
+  refusal is checked on `rollback` too);
+- the distribution the workflow serves is not recorded — `target
+  distribution <d> is not recorded`;
 - the workflow already serves the source — `<w> already serves <d>`;
-- a task's step does not exist in the target — `N task(s) cannot move: <id>:
-  <step> does not exist in the target`;
-- an open task's runtime identity pin is unreadable — `cannot read the pin of
-  N open task(s)`;
-- a task's pin and its position name different workflows — `N open task(s)
-  name different workflows in their pin and their position`;
-- receipts are already open — `N migrations are still open for <w>`;
-- `--from` does not match the open receipt — `open migration <id> moves from
-  <from>, not <f>`;
+- a task's step does not exist in the target — `N task(s) cannot move:
+  <id> (step <s> does not exist in the target)`;
+- the planned migration was already sealed — `migration <id> is already
+  sealed; <rid> reversed it` when a sealed receipt reverses it, else
+  `migration <id> is already sealed and nothing has reversed it`; the
+  advice after ` — ` is `autosk migrate rollback <wid> moves the tasks
+  again` when a rollback qualifies (`<wid>` names that receipt and can
+  differ from `<rid>`), else `no single rollback moves these tasks onto
+  <to> now` (`rollback` checks the same condition).
+
+`apply` also declines while a receipt is open:
+
+- several migrations are open — `N migrations are still open for <w>
+  (<ids>)`;
+- `--from` does not match the open receipt — `open migration <id> moves
+  from <from>, not <f>`;
 - the open receipt's target is not served any more, in any of its three
   recorded components — `open migration <id> targets <to>, but <w> serves
-  <served>` (distribution), `open migration <id> declares graph <g>, but <w>
-  serves <g'>` (declared shape), or `open migration <id> declares document
-  <d>, but <w> serves <d'>` (target document; checked only when the receipt
-  records one), or `has no identified distribution` when the workflow serves
-  nothing.
+  <served>` (distribution), `open migration <id> declares graph <g>, but
+  <w> serves <g'>` (declared shape), or `open migration <id> declares
+  document <d>, but <w> serves <d'>` (target document; checked only when
+  the receipt records one);
+- the workflow serves nothing — `open migration <id> cannot continue: <w>
+  has no identified distribution`.
 
 `rollback` declines when:
 
 - the id names no migration — `migration <id> was never opened`;
 - the prefix matches several — `migration id <id> is ambiguous (N matches)`;
-- the migration was never sealed — `migration <id> is not completed, there is
-  nothing settled to reverse`;
-- the registry still serves the migrated-to distribution, or serves neither —
-  `rolling back <id> needs <w> to serve <from>; it serves <served>`;
-- several reversals of the same migration are open — `N rollbacks of <id> are
-  still open`;
+- the migration was never sealed — `migration <id> is not completed, there
+  is nothing settled to reverse`;
+- the reverse plan's receipt is already sealed — the same `migration <id>
+  is already sealed` refusals listed for `plan`/`apply` above;
+- the registry still serves the migrated-to distribution, or serves neither
+  — `rolling back <id> needs <w> to serve <from>; it serves <served>, so
+  the registry does not serve the distribution it came from`;
+- the registry serves the right distribution under a different document —
+  `rolling back <id> needs <w> to serve document <d>; it serves <d'>`;
+- the served document is malformed — the same `serves a document that is
+  not a lowercase sha256` refusal listed for `plan`/`apply` above;
+- several reversals of the same migration are open — `N rollbacks of <id>
+  are still open (<ids>)`;
